#synthesis(title: [Convergent Genetic Evidence on Neuronal Biology])[
Two largely independent data types converge on neuronal biology as the genetic substrate of ME/CFS. (1) Common variant GWAS (@DecodeME2025, Maccallini 2026 meta-GWAS @Maccallini2026metaGWAS) enriches in brain tissues and neuronal gene-sets and, at cell-type resolution, in neurons rather than in the CNS-resident immune cells (microglia; the brain-only atlases do not test peripheral-immune cell types); the specific top subtype differs by atlas and modality: the RNA references point to striatal MSNs (Human Brain Atlas, @Siletti2023) or inhibitory interneurons (DESCARTES fetal, @Lee2026geneticOverlap), while the chromatin atlas points to intratelencephalic cortical neurons @Li2023ATACbrain (same-analyst, unpublished). (2) Rare variant burden from whole-exome sequencing concentrates in neuronal and synaptic genes @Snyder2025rareBrain.

This two-component genetic architecture (brain-enriched common variants + rare neuronal variants) has one principal implication and one important caveat. The principal implication is that it provides converging support for the brain-first genetic model: genetic liability is statistically concentrated in neuronal circuits, consistent with peripheral pathology arising downstream rather than from a primary peripheral genetic risk (these data do not establish the causal direction). The important caveat is the immune null — no significant enrichment in any immune cell type when tested against the *mouse* ImmGen reference via the Finucane 2018 stratified LDSC pipeline, in a single unpublished, power-limited analysis — does not contradict the extensive evidence for immune dysfunction in ME/CFS documented in Chapter @ch:immune-dysfunction. It is *consistent with*, but does not establish, the reading that immune pathology is acquired (infection-triggered) rather than encoded in common variants: a power-limited, single-reference negative cannot distinguish acquired immune pathology from common-variant immune liability falling below the detection threshold (or sitting in rare variants, or in immune cell types absent from the mouse reference).

GWAS cell-type enrichment tells us *where constitutional liability is encoded* (neurons), not which mechanisms are operative in the established disease. All trigger mechanisms — viral infection, GPCR autoantibody production, mast cell activation, TRPM3 channelopathy, skeletal asymmetry — and all amplifier mechanisms — the PEM metabolic danger cascade, kindling-driven threshold lowering, glymphatic failure, complement-mast cell amplification loops, endothelial senescence, NET/DNase imbalance — remain valid acquired pathophysiological processes. The brain-first genetic architecture does not displace them; it constrains the upstream origin of vulnerability while the triggers and amplifiers describe the downstream acquired pathology that propagates from or triggers upon that vulnerable CNS substrate. This is formalised in the two-hit model: genetic vulnerability in neuronal circuits (hit 1) + environmental trigger (hit 2) → disease, with amplifiers sustaining chronicity. Neither invalidates the other.

However, cell-type enrichment at the MSN/eMSN level is method-dependent (certainty ~0.40–0.50; the 0.80 figure applies only to brain *tissue*-level enrichment, not to cell-type resolution), and MSN enrichment is shared across multiple brain-relevant traits (schizophrenia, depression, alcohol consumption, sleep duration — @Duncan2025cellTypeBrain). The genetic data identify *where* ME/CFS risk is encoded (neurons), and hint at a specific subtype (MSNs in some RNA references, intratelencephalic cortical neurons in the chromatin atlas), but do not yet identify *which mechanism* within those neurons mediates risk. The most important open question is whether the genetic signal points to a specific MSN-intrinsic pathology or to a broader neuronal process that MSN transcriptomes happen to tag by their high transcriptional diversity.

*Consequence:* Multiple partially overlapping methods using different data types are consistent with ME/CFS risk being genetically encoded in neurons rather than the CNS-resident immune cells (microglia); the human atlases are brain-only and cannot test peripheral-immune enrichment, and the only immune cell-type test was a power-limited mouse-reference null (the preprint meta-GWAS found no peripheral *tissue*-level enrichment, but tissue-level nullity does not establish cell-type-level absence) — so the finding, if sustained, would redirect research toward neuronal-circuit mechanisms without showing that peripheral immune dysfunction is uninvolved. For patients: it supports a genetic, biological contribution to ME/CFS, expressed in brain cell types.
] <syn:genetic-neuronal-convergence>
